17. DEBUG FRONTEND -> LARAVEL API
=================================

Example frontend request:

fetch('/api/products', {
method: 'POST',
headers: {
'Content-Type': 'application/json',
'Accept': 'application/json'
},
body: JSON.stringify({
name: 'Laptop',
price: 50000
})
});

Explanation:

The frontend sends JSON data to Laravel.

---

Better debugging version:

const data = {
name: 'Laptop',
price: 50000
};

console.log('Sending:', data);

const response = await fetch('/api/products', {
method: 'POST',
headers: {
'Content-Type': 'application/json',
'Accept': 'application/json'
},
body: JSON.stringify(data)
});

console.log('Status:', response.status);

const result = await response.json();

console.log('Response:', result);

Explanation:

This lets you see:

1. What frontend is sending.
2. HTTP response status.
3. What Laravel returned.

==================================================
18. DEBUG THE SAME REQUEST IN LARAVEL
=====================================

Controller:

public function store(Request $request)
{
dd([
'headers' => $request->headers->all(),
'data' => $request->all(),
]);
}

Explanation:

Now compare:

Frontend data
↓
Browser Network Payload
↓
Laravel received data

If the data is already wrong when it reaches Laravel,
the problem is likely in the frontend/request.

If the data is correct, continue debugging the backend.

==================================================
19. DEBUG BLADE
===============

Inside Blade:

{{ dd($products) }}

Explanation:

Displays the value and stops execution.

---

Without stopping:

{{ dump($products) }}

Explanation:

Displays the value and continues execution.

---

Display structured data:

<pre>
    {{ print_r($products, true) }}
</pre>

Explanation:

Useful for visually inspecting arrays/objects in a Blade page.

---

Display JSON:

<pre>
    {{ json_encode($products, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT) }}
</pre>

Explanation:

Useful for seeing data in JSON format.

==================================================
20. DEBUG PHP DATA PASSED TO JAVASCRIPT
=======================================

Example:

<script>
    const products = @json($products);

    console.log('Products:', products);
</script>

Explanation:

Laravel/PHP data is converted into JavaScript data.

Then you can inspect it using:

console.log(products);

---

Alternative:

<script>
    const products = {{ Js::from($products) }};

    console.log(products);
</script>

Explanation:

Useful for safely passing Laravel data into JavaScript.

==================================================
21. DEBUG AXIOS REQUESTS
========================

Example:

axios.post('/api/products', {
name: 'Laptop',
price: 50000
})
.then(response => {
console.log('Success:', response.data);
})
.catch(error => {
console.error('Status:', error.response?.status);
console.error('Data:', error.response?.data);
});

Explanation:

If the request succeeds:

response.data

contains Laravel's response.

If the request fails:

error.response.status

contains the HTTP status.

error.response.data

contains the server response.

---

DEBUG VALIDATION ERRORS:

.catch(error => {
if (error.response?.status === 422) {
console.log(error.response.data.errors);
}
});

Explanation:

This specifically detects Laravel validation errors.

==================================================
22. DEBUG AUTHENTICATION
========================

Use:

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Auth;

dd(Auth::user());

Explanation:

Displays the currently authenticated user.

If it returns:

null

the request is not authenticated according to the
current authentication setup.

---

Check authentication:

dd([
'authenticated' => Auth::check(),
'user' => Auth::user(),
]);

Explanation:

Shows:

authenticated
Whether the user is authenticated.

user
The authenticated user.

---

Using the request:

dd($request->user());

Explanation:

Returns the authenticated user associated with the request.

==================================================
23. DEBUG API TOKEN AUTHENTICATION
==================================

Open browser:

F12
↓
Network
↓
Your API request
↓
Headers

Check whether the request contains the expected
authentication information.

For token-based APIs, this may look like:

Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN

Explanation:

If authentication is failing, inspect the actual request
being sent by the browser.

==================================================
24. DEBUG CSRF / 419 ERRORS
===========================

If you receive:

419 Page Expired

Check CSRF/session handling.

For a Blade form:

<form method="POST" action="/products">

```
@csrf

...
```

</form>

Explanation:

@csrf generates the CSRF token required for protected
web form requests.

---

IMPORTANT:

Do NOT simply disable CSRF protection to solve a 419 error.

Instead, investigate:

* CSRF token
* Session
* Cookies
* Request headers
* Route type
* Authentication
* Domain configuration

==================================================
25. DEBUG WITH BREAKPOINTS
==========================

For more advanced debugging, use Xdebug with your IDE.

Instead of:

dd($user);

you can place a breakpoint:

public function store(Request $request)
{
$user = $request->user();

```
// BREAKPOINT HERE

$product = Product::create([
    'name' => $request->name,
]);
```

}

Explanation:

When execution reaches the breakpoint, your debugger pauses
the PHP application.

You can inspect:

$request
$user
$product

---

Common debugger actions:

Step Over
Execute the current line and move to the next line.

Step Into
Enter the function being called.

Step Out
Exit the current function.

Continue
Continue execution until another breakpoint or the
request finishes.

